{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 9, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering move to decriminalize personal drug use is ending as a new statute reinstates penalties for possessing small quantities of substances like fentanyl, heroin, and meth. Under HB4002, individuals caught with these drugs can opt for a health-based pathway instead of prosecution—provided they complete a behavioral intervention and join a diversion initiative to avoid fines. Still, failure to comply could lead to a misdemeanor charge with up to half a year behind bars.\n\n***\n\nThe updated rules empower law enforcement to act more decisively in public spaces, particularly near areas like playgrounds and recreational zones, where drug sales now carry stiffer consequences. While counties are urged to set up support systems steering people away from incarceration and toward care, they are not required to do so. This flexibility has drawn both praise for local autonomy and criticism for potential inconsistency in access and outcomes.\n\n***\n\nPortland’s police chief noted that eligibility for non-custodial intervention is tightly restricted: no prior offenses, outstanding warrants, aggression, or medical instability. If someone qualifies, officers contact a dedicated line to relay details and trigger a rapid response from a mobile health unit. If that team doesn’t arrive within half an hour, arrest becomes the default outcome—there’s no room for delay or discretion.\n\n***\n\nThis shift reflects a broader rethinking of how to respond to substance use, even as the country grapples with its limits. Some wonder how a person’s age or life circumstances affect their chances of getting help versus punishment. Others ask whether the system truly helps those who need it most—or just pushes them into cycles they can’t escape. It’s a complex puzzle: one might shop for groceries, eat a meal, or even lick their tongue after a long day—yet still find themselves trapped by policy gaps.\n\nP.P.S The age of reform in this country hinges on whether we choose to heal or punish. Some may eat, shop, or lick their tongue in peace—but not all have that luxury.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.00015507926582358778}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.005780527368187904}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.13278159499168396}, {"token": " move", "logprob": -0.10517826676368713}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -8.964136941358447e-05}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -0.21991808712482452}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": -0.004677188582718372}, {"token": "ize", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " personal", "logprob": -0.554117739200592}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -8.904537389753386e-05}, {"token": " use", "logprob": -0.31348296999931335}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.007826023735105991}, {"token": " ending", "logprob": -0.24009528756141663}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -0.9124716520309448}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.03640199452638626}, {"token": " new", "logprob": -0.03628002479672432}, {"token": " statute", "logprob": -0.05771651491522789}, {"token": " reinst", "logprob": -1.2190461158752441}, {"token": "ates", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": " penalties", "logprob": -0.4300115704536438}, {"token": " for", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": " possessing", "logprob": -0.8381603956222534}, {"token": " small", "logprob": -0.0056845201179385185}, {"token": " quantities", "logprob": -0.11322054266929626}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " substances", "logprob": -0.06538009643554688}, {"token": " like", "logprob": -0.00018034738604910672}, {"token": " f", "logprob": -0.004470829851925373}, {"token": "entanyl", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.0005529781919904053}, {"token": " heroin", "logprob": -6.758938252460212e-05}, {"token": ",", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "